|~ What is declared under `::number`: its constructor and the refusals of
   a payload of another kind and of an answer that leaves the doubles,
   and the words that run on a number, each told in one order: the word
   and its page, its answer, its parts, its refusals, its laws. ~|

:number/construct
  |~~ Reads a number as itself, a finite double. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/PayloadNotNumberError
  |~~ `::number` reads its payload as the number it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused, and text is read by `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/PayloadNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string payload is refused; `parseJson` reads a number from text. ~~|
  ::law~(::number"5" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/PayloadNotNumberError ::string])

:number/NotFiniteError
  |~~ A number is a finite double: an answer brought under `::number`
      that left their range, a sum or a product too large, is refused
      here, whichever word computed it, and the trail names the step and
      the subject it ran on.
  ~~|

:number/NotFiniteError/refusesAProductPastTheRange
  |~~ A product past the range of doubles is refused at the step that made it. ~~|
  ::law~(1e308 | mul 10 | add 1 !| [type (/trail * /step)] | eq [::number/NotFiniteError [~(mul 10)]])

:number/add
  |~~ Answers the subject plus the number the call gives, `10 | add 3`.
      The subject is a number: a vector adds to each of its elements under
      `*`, `* add 1`, and sums them by `sum`; a string joins by `append`;
      and text that holds a number is read first by `parseJson`, `"5" |
      parseJson | add 1`. The addend is computed against the subject, so
      `5 | add (mul 2)` adds ten, and two fields of one record add by
      `sumOf /x /y`. A number under a tag answers under the tag,
      `::Width(2) | add 3` answering `::Width(5)`, and a sum past the
      doubles is refused as no number.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/add/answer
  |~~ The subject and the addend added, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/add/addend
  |~~ The number added to the subject, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/add/addend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The addend is a number; text that holds one is read by `parseJson`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/add/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `add` adds to a number: two fields of one record add by `sumOf /x
      /y`, a vector adds to each element under `*`, `* add 1`, a string
      joins by `append`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/add/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `add` adds one number to the subject: the numbers of a vector sum
      by `sum`, `[1 2 3] | sum`, and two fields of one record add by
      `sumOf /x /y`.
  ~~|

:number/add/addsTheNumber
  |~~ `add` answers the sum. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)

:number/add/computesTheAddendAgainstTheSubject
  |~~ The addend is computed against the subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | add (mul 2) | eq 15)

:number/add/refusesASumPastTheDoubles
  |~~ A sum past the doubles is refused as no number. ~~|
  ::law~(1e308 | add 1e308 !| type | eq ::number/NotFiniteError)

:number/add/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | add 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/add/addend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ An addend that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/addend/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/add/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoNumbers
  |~~ Args of two numbers are refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | add 1 2 !| type | eq ::number/add/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:number/sub
  |~~ Answers the subject, the minuend, less the number the call gives,
      `10 | sub 3`, which may fall below zero, `5 | sub 7` answering `-2`.
      The subject is a number: a vector subtracts from each of its
      elements under `*`, `* sub 1`, and text that holds a number is read
      first by `parseJson`. The subtrahend is computed against the
      subject, and two fields of one record subtract by `subOf /total
      /paid`. A number under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/add | doc) ::link~(::any/subOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/sub/answer
  |~~ The subject less the subtrahend, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/sub/subtrahend
  |~~ The number subtracted from the subject, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `sub` subtracts from a number: two fields of one record subtract by
      `subOf /total /paid`, a vector subtracts from each element under
      `*`, `* sub 1`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/sub/subtractsTheNumber
  |~~ `sub` answers the difference. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)

:number/sub/fallsBelowZero
  |~~ A difference may fall below zero. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | sub 7 | eq -2)

:number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | sub 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subtrahend that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | sub "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/mul
  |~~ Answers the subject times the number the call gives, `10 | mul 3`,
      a fraction among them, `7 | mul 0.5` answering `3.5`. The subject
      is a number: a vector multiplies each of its elements under `*`,
      `* mul 2`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`. The multiplier is computed against the subject, so
      `5 | mul /` squares it, and two fields of one record multiply by
      `mulOf /price /qty`. A number under a tag answers under the tag,
      and a product past the doubles is refused as no number.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/mul/answer
  |~~ The subject times the multiplier, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/mul/multiplier
  |~~ The number the subject is multiplied by, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError
  |~~ The multiplier is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `mul` multiplies a number: two fields of one record multiply by
      `mulOf /price /qty`, a vector multiplies each element under `*`,
      `* mul 2`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/mul/multipliesByTheNumber
  |~~ `mul` answers the product. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)

:number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | mul 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A multiplier that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | mul "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/div
  |~~ Answers the subject, the dividend, divided by the number the call
      gives, a fraction where the division leaves one, `7 | div 2`
      answering `3.5`. A divisor of zero is refused, so a divisor that may
      be zero is tested first, `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum
      /count)`. The subject is a number: a vector divides each of its
      elements under `*`, `* div 2`. The divisor is computed against the
      subject, and two fields of one record divide by `divOf /sum
      /count`. A number under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/mul | doc) ::link~(::any/divOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/div/answer
  |~~ The subject divided by the divisor, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::number

:number/div/divisor
  |~~ The number the subject is divided by, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/div/divisor/NotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/div/divisor/ZeroError
  |~~ The divisor is not zero: a division by zero has no finite answer,
      and a divisor that may be zero is tested first,
      `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum /count)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq 0 | not)

:number/div/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `div` divides a number: two fields of one record divide by `divOf
      /sum /count`, a vector divides each element under `*`, `* div 2`,
      and text that holds a number is read first by `parseJson`.
  ~~|

:number/div/dividesByTheNumber
  |~~ `div` answers the quotient. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)

:number/div/leavesAFraction
  |~~ A division that leaves a fraction answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(7 | div 2 | eq 3.5)

:number/div/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | div 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/div/divisor/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | div "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/divisor/NotNumberError ::string])

:number/div/divisor/ZeroError/refusesZero
  |~~ A division by zero is refused under the divisor. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | div 0 !| type | eq ::number/div/divisor/ZeroError)

:number/round
  |~~ Answers the subject rounded to the digits after the point the call
      gives, none when it gives none: `3.14159 | round 2` answers `3.14`
      and `2.5 | round` answers `3`. A half rounds away from zero, as the
      digits are written, `1.005 | round 2` answering `1.01` and `-2.5 |
      round` answering `-3`; a negative count rounds to tens and beyond,
      `1234 | round -2` answering `1200`; and a number rounded to zero
      prints as `0`. The digits are a whole number. The subject is a
      number, and text that holds one is read first by `parseJson`.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/round/answer
  |~~ The subject rounded to the digits. ~~|
  ::number

:number/round/digits
  |~~ The digits after the point, a whole number, none when the call
      gives none.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0 0)

:number/round/digits/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count of digits is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError
  |~~ The digits of `round` are a whole number, `round 2`. ~~|

:number/round/SubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `round` rounds a number: text that holds one is read first by
      `parseJson`, `"2.5" | parseJson | round`.
  ~~|

:number/round/keepsTheDigitsGiven
  |~~ Two digits keep two places after the point. ~~|
  ::law~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)

:number/round/roundsAHalfAwayFromZero
  |~~ A half rounds away from zero. ~~|
  ::law~(2.5 | round | eq 3)

:number/round/roundsANegativeHalfAwayFromZero
  |~~ A negative half rounds away from zero too. ~~|
  ::law~(-2.5 | round | eq -3)

:number/round/roundsTheDigitsAsWritten
  |~~ A half rounds as the digits are written. ~~|
  ::law~(1.005 | round 2 | eq 1.01)

:number/round/roundsToHundreds
  |~~ A negative count rounds to tens and beyond. ~~|
  ::law~(1234 | round -2 | eq 1200)

:number/round/printsNoNegativeZero
  |~~ A negative number rounded to zero prints as 0. ~~|
  ::law~(-0.4 | round | print | eq "0")

:number/round/SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to round. ~~|
  ::law~("2.5" | round !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])

:number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ Half a digit is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(2.5 | round 0.5 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError ::number])

:number/gt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is greater than the number the call
      gives, `10 | gt 5`, a decimal against a whole number among them,
      `3 | gt 2.5`. Numbers compare with numbers: a string, a keyword, a
      tag name and a value under `::desc` each compare with their own
      kind by a `gt` of their own, and a vector, a set, a map or null
      answers no `gt`, which the refusal of the call names with the
      kinds that do. The value is computed against the subject, and in a
      condition the subject is the element, `filter ~(gt 1)`. `gte`
      answers whether the subject is greater or equal, `lt` and `lte`
      the reverse.
      ::link~(::number/gte | doc) ::link~(::number/lt | doc)
      ::link~(::number/lte | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/gt/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gt/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gt/comparesNumbers
  |~~ Ten is greater than five. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | gt 5)

:number/gt/comparesADecimal
  |~~ A whole number compares with a decimal. ~~|
  ::law~(3 | gt 2.5)

:number/gt/keepsOneNumberApartFromItself
  |~~ A number is not greater than itself. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | gt 5 | not)

:number/gt/filtersByTheElement
  |~~ In a condition the subject is the element. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1) | eq [2 3])

:number/gt/value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | gt "a" !| type | eq ::number/gt/value/NotNumberError)

:number/gte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number
      the call gives, `10 | gte 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers
      with numbers, a whole number with a decimal, `5 | gte 5.0`
      answering `true`.
      ::link~(::number/gt | doc) ::link~(::number/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value or equals it, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/gte/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gte/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A number is greater than or equal to itself. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | gte 10)

:number/gte/holdsOfItsDecimal
  |~~ A whole number equals its decimal. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | gte 5.0)

:number/lt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is less than the number the call gives,
      `5 | lt 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers with numbers, and
      `lte` answers whether the subject is less or equal.
      ::link~(::number/gt | doc) ::link~(::number/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lt/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lt/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lt/comparesNumbers
  |~~ Five is less than ten. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | lt 10)

:number/lt/keepsOneNumberApartFromItself
  |~~ A number is not less than itself. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | lt 5 | not)

:number/lte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the
      call gives, `10 | lte 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers with
      numbers.
      ::link~(::number/lt | doc) ::link~(::number/gte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value or equals it, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lte/value
  |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lte/value/NotNumberError
  |~~ The value compared is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A number is less than or equal to itself. ~~|
  ::law~(10 | lte 10)
